    "key": "q2",
    "subject": "The wall, and the 3 steps over it",
    "html": "<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\"><meta name=\"color-scheme\" content=\"light\"><title>Child Phenoms</title></head>\n<body style=\"margin:0;padding:0;background:#f6f7fb;color:#121826;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\">\n<div style=\"display:none;max-height:0;overflow:hidden;opacity:0\">Where most families quit, and how not to.</div>\n<table role=\"presentation\" width=\"100%\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"background:#f6f7fb\"><tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:24px 12px\">\n<table role=\"presentation\" width=\"100%\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"max-width:560px;background:#ffffff;border-radius:16px\"><tr><td style=\"padding:28px 26px 24px\">\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 22px;font-weight:800;font-size:18px;color:#121826\">Child <span style=\"color:#2457f5\">Phenoms</span></p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Hi {{first_name}},</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Did you do Night 1? If not, tonight is fine. Here is the plan I promised.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Every child hits a wall in math. For most kids it comes around third grade. Counting on fingers stops working. The numbers get too big. School answers with more memorizing. Flash cards. Timed tests. Tears at the kitchen table.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Only 28 of every 100 eighth graders in the US scored proficient in math on the 2024 national test. School is not going to fix this for your child.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">For me the wall came later. I wanted to play college sports and my grades were the wall. The beads got me over it.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">The abacus takes a different road. Three steps. Every abacus child in Japan walks them, in this order.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\"><b>Step 1: See it.</b> Your child learns what each bead means. One. Five. Ten, on the next rod. Numbers become a picture you can point to.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\"><b>Step 2: Move it.</b> The hands do the sums. Add 1, push a bead. Add 5, pull the top bead down. The hands learn the rules before the head has to.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\"><b>Step 3: Imagine it.</b> The abacus goes away. Your child closes their eyes and moves the beads in their mind. That is the mental calculator. That is the kid on the stage.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Here is where families quit: between step 1 and step 2. Week two. The new wears off. The friend moves feel hard. Parents think it is not working.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">It is working. Around week three it clicks. It did for me.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Here is why I do not let families quit there. Each step pulls in more of the brain. Step 1 is eyes. Step 2 adds hands. Step 3 is pure picture, inside the head. By the end, your child solves with parts of the brain most kids never use for math. That is what I mean by whole brain.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">And it shows up far from math. In the five-year study, it showed up as better memory for shapes and space. In my house, it showed up as a kid who stopped fearing tests.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Your job this week is steps 1 and 2 only. Tonight is Night 2: two-digit numbers. Stage 1 in your account has the rest of steps 1 and 2, and it picks each problem for you.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Tomorrow: the exact words to say at the table, and the one mistake that makes kids hate it.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">Hunter</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55\">P.S. Night 2 is on the screen abacus here: <a href=\"https://childphenoms.com/#/stage/1\" style=\"color:#2457f5;text-decoration:underline\">childphenoms.com/#/stage/1</a></p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:34px;padding-top:16px;border-top:1px solid #e6e8ef;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#6b7280\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">Child Phenoms, a DBA of Enamelogic LLC · 1309 Coffeen Avenue STE 1200, Sheridan, WY 82801 · hello@childphenoms.com</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">You get this because you ordered a kit or started an order at childphenoms.com. Unsubscribe: {{unsubscribe}}</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">Research cited describes group averages in published trials. Your child's progress depends on practice.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">Instructor earnings are not promised. What you charge is up to you.</p></div>\n</td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n</body></html>",
    "text": "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nDid you do Night 1? If not, tonight is fine. Here is the plan I promised.\n\nEvery child hits a wall in math. For most kids it comes around third grade. Counting on fingers stops working. The numbers get too big. School answers with more memorizing. Flash cards. Timed tests. Tears at the kitchen table.\n\nOnly 28 of every 100 eighth graders in the US scored proficient in math on the 2024 national test. School is not going to fix this for your child.\n\nFor me the wall came later. I wanted to play college sports and my grades were the wall. The beads got me over it.\n\nThe abacus takes a different road. Three steps. Every abacus child in Japan walks them, in this order.\n\nStep 1: See it. Your child learns what each bead means. One. Five. Ten, on the next rod. Numbers become a picture you can point to.\n\nStep 2: Move it. The hands do the sums. Add 1, push a bead. Add 5, pull the top bead down. The hands learn the rules before the head has to.\n\nStep 3: Imagine it. The abacus goes away. Your child closes their eyes and moves the beads in their mind. That is the mental calculator. That is the kid on the stage.\n\nHere is where families quit: between step 1 and step 2. Week two. The new wears off. The friend moves feel hard. Parents think it is not working.\n\nIt is working. Around week three it clicks. It did for me.\n\nHere is why I do not let families quit there. Each step pulls in more of the brain. Step 1 is eyes. Step 2 adds hands. Step 3 is pure picture, inside the head. By the end, your child solves with parts of the brain most kids never use for math. That is what I mean by whole brain.\n\nAnd it shows up far from math. In the five-year study, it showed up as better memory for shapes and space. In my house, it showed up as a kid who stopped fearing tests.\n\nYour job this week is steps 1 and 2 only. Tonight is Night 2: two-digit numbers. Stage 1 in your account has the rest of steps 1 and 2, and it picks each problem for you.\n\nTomorrow: the exact words to say at the table, and the one mistake that makes kids hate it.\n\nHunter\n\nP.S. Night 2 is on the screen abacus here: https://childphenoms.com/#/stage/1\n\n— — —\nChild Phenoms, a DBA of Enamelogic LLC · 1309 Coffeen Avenue STE 1200, Sheridan, WY 82801 · hello@childphenoms.com\nYou get this because you ordered a kit or started an order at childphenoms.com. Unsubscribe: {{unsubscribe}}\nResearch cited describes group averages in published trials. Your child's progress depends on practice.\nInstructor earnings are not promised. What you charge is up to you."
